components.readme:
The `arcgis-configuration-editor` is a json-driven form-editing component that allows us to declaratively build form-based UIs.

1. [Technical Implementation](#technical-implementation)
   - [editor changes](#editor-changes)
   - [validation](#validation)
     - [custom validation](#custom-validation)
2. [Further Details](#further-details)
   - [schema](#schema)
   - [uiSchema](#uischema)
     - [types](#types)
     - [rules](#rules)

## Technical Implementation

The `arcgis-configuration-editor` renders form fields based on:

1. a data/JSON `schema` which defines _what_ the underlying data properties are. The `schema` should follow [JSON Schema](https://json-schema.org/) format:

   ```ts
   const schema = {
     type: "object",
     requried: [ "name" ]
     properties: {
       name: {
         type: "string",
         maxLength: 255,
         ...
       }
     }
   }
   ```

2. an optional `uiSchema` which defines _how_ the data properties are rendered. The `uiSchema` is based on concepts from [jsonforms.io](https://jsonforms.io/):

   ```ts
   const uiSchema = {
     type: "Layout",
     elements: [
       {
         scope: "/properties/name",
         type: "Control",
         labelKey: 'name.label',
         control: "hub-field-input-input",
         options: {
           helperText: { labelKey: 'name.helperText' },
           ...
         }
       }
     ]
   }
   ```

The `arcgis-configuration-editor` iterates over the `uiSchema` elements and renders an `arcgis-configuration-editor-field` component for each property.

The `arcgis-configuration-editor-field` in turn renders the appropriate field component based on the control declared in the `uiSchema`.

Each field component is in turn a thin wrapper around another UI element (typically a `calcite` input element) to make it functional in the context of the configuration editor.

### Editor Changes

When the user updates any value in the editor, the `arcgis-configuration-editor` emits a custom event, `arcgisConfigurationEditorChange`. The event detail includes the validation result and all current configuration values:

```ts
{
  detail: {
    valid: true|false,
    values: {
      // ... key-value pairs
    }
  }
}
```

### Validation

We use [AJV](https://www.npmjs.com/package/ajv) for schema validation. On every editor change event, we validate the editor's values holistically against the schema. If there's any value violating the property schema rules, we emit `valid: false` in the `arcgisConfigurationEditorChange` event, and the `arcgis-configuration-editor-field` is passed an `invalid` property which gets passed down to the corresponding field to handle how it chooses.

#### Custom Validation

Beyond AJV's [built-in formats](https://ajv.js.org/guide/formats.html), you can [define your own validation formats](./utils/formats/readme.md) to use in your schema. You can also apply custom validation logic to a field in your schema by [defining a keyword](./utils/keywords/readme.md).

## Required fields logic

The configuration editor has technical complexity when dealing with required fields: a summary of the logic is below.

The configuration editor creates three state properties: `alwaysRequired`, `conditionallyRequired`, and `currentlyRequired`.

- `alwaysRequired` and `conditionallyRequired` are set in `componentWillLoad` and whenever the schema changes.

- `currentlyRequired` is set in those two scenarios and ALSO when a field changes.

- `alwaysRequired` is an array of properties that are explicitly required by the schema, and that do not change. This means they are set in a `required` array within the schema, outside of a conditional.

- `conditionallyRequired` is a map of properties. The key is an `if` property, and the value is a set of properties that _could_ be required by the schema, as they are found inside of a conditional (the `then/else` clause related to the `if`). Currently, the only implemented place is inside of the `allOf` of the schema.

- `currentlyRequired` is a Set of properties from `alwaysRequired` and `conditionallyRequired` that are required at any given point in time. This means that this array will have all of the `alwaysRequired` properties, and could have all, some, or none of the `conditionallyRequired` properties.

We find the `alwaysRequired` properties by running an empty version of the schema values against the validator. Note that the values should follow the structure of the schema and not just be an empty object.

We find the `conditionallyRequired` properties by parsing the if/then/else clauses inside of the `allOf`. We set any properties mentioned in the `if` clause as a key, and all properties mentioned in the `required` array of the `then/else` clause as the value. Note that there is also one special key, `allConditionallyRequiredProperties`, that has a set of all of the properties in the map's values.

We find the `currentlyRequired` properties depending on if we are initializing the set or if we are updating the set based on a property change.

If we are initializing, we first add all of the `alwaysRequired` properties. Then, for every property in `conditionallyRequired.get("allConditionallyRequiredProperties")`, we delete the property from the current values, run it against the validator, and see if an error is produced. If there is an error, we add it to the `currentlyRequired` properties.

If we are checking a specific property because of a field change, we instead run `conditionallyRequired.get(propertyName)`. For every property in this value, we delete the property from the current values, run it against the validator, and see if an error is produced. If there is an error, we add it to the `currentlyRequired` properties. If not, we remove it if it exists from the `currentlyRequired` properties.

## Further Details

### `schema`

The `schema` describes _what_ should be rendered in the editor. It follows JSON Schema format. This allows us to easily validate data passed into the configuration editor (as detailed above). Reference the [JSON Schema Docs](https://json-schema.org/understanding-json-schema/) for more information.

### `uiSchema`

The optional `uiSchema` describes _how_ the editor should be rendered. The `uiSchema` does not have a validation specification like JSON schema; however, we drew inspiration from [jsonforms.io](https://jsonforms.io/).

If a `uiSchema` is not provided to the component, a basic default `uiSchema` will be generated based on the `schema`. If a `uiSchema` is provided, only properties defined in both the `schema` and the `uiSchema` will be rendered.

#### `IUiSchema`

The `uiSchema` implements `IUiSchema`. It is an `IUiSchemaElement` with an additional property, `elements` which is an array of `IUiSchemaElement`s.

`IUiSchemaElement` has the following properties:

| Properties | Type               | Required?                                                         | Description                                                                                                                                      |
| ---------- | ------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `type`     | `string`           | yes                                                               | The type of `uiSchemaElement`. Possible values are `Layout`, `Section`, `Control`, `Slot`.                                                       |
| `scope`    | `string`           | no (yes for controls)                                             | A jsonpointer string that points to/identifies which property in the `schema` this element of the `uiSchema` pertains to.                        |
| `label`    | `string`           | no (either `labelKey` or `label` should be supplied for controls) | An untranslated label for the control or section.                                                                                                |
| `labelKey` | `string`           | no (either `labelKey` or `label` should be supplied for controls) | An i18n key with which to produce a translated label for the control or section.                                                                 |
| `rule`     | `IUiSchemaRule`    | no                                                                | NOTE: this is deprecated in favor of rules. A `uiSchema` rule that specifies whether this control should be shown, hidden, enabled, or disabled. |
| `rules`    | `IUiSchemaRule[]`  | no                                                                | An array of `uiSchema` rules that specify whether this control should be shown, hidden, enabled, disabled, or reset.                             |
| `options`  | `IUiSchemaOptions` | no                                                                | An options object for the control or section. This object is specific to the control/section. See individual field docs for more info            |

#### types

##### `type = "Control"`

A `uiSchema` element of `type="Control"` allows us to render individual form fields. See individual field docs for more info

##### `type = "Section"`

A `uiSchema` element of `type = "Section"` allows us to render forms in various section formats:

1. `basic`

   ```ts
   const uiSchema = {
     type: 'Section',
     options: {
       headerTag: 'h1'|'h2'|'h3'|'h4'|'h5'|'h6'| // underlying semantic header tag to be used for the section
       requiredHelperText: {
         label: "*indicates required field" // renders custom helper text at the top of the section to indicate what to do for required fields. "*indicates required field" renders by default if a top-level section and has required fields.
       }
     }
   }
   ```

2. `block`

   ```ts
   const uiSchema = {
     type: 'Section',
     options: {
       section: 'block'
       open: true | false // whether the block section should default to being open,
       requiredHelperText: {
         label: "*indicates required field" // renders custom helper text at the top of the section to indicate what to do for required fields. "*indicates required field" renders by default if a top-level section and has required fields.
       }
     }
   }
   ```

   _NOTE:_ This section should NOT be used as of now as it does not pass a11y standards due to a calcite bug. Instead, use the accordion section.

3. `stepper`

   ```ts
   const uiSchema = {
     type: 'Section',
     options: {
       section: 'stepper',
       elements: [
         {
           type: 'Step',
           elements: [ ... ],
           options: {
             requiredHelperText: {
               label: "*indicates required field" // renders custom helper text at the top of the section to indicate what to do for required fields. "*indicates required field" renders by default if a top-level section and has required fields.
             }
           }
         }
       ]
     }
   }
   ```

4. `subblock`

   ```ts
   const uiSchema = {
     type: 'Section',
     options: {
       section: 'subblock',
       open: true | false, // whether the subblock should default to being open
       toggleDisplay: 'button' | 'switch', // render the block section with a switch or a button (default)
       requiredHelperText: {
         label: '*indicates required field', // renders custom helper text at the top of the section to indicate what to do for required fields. "*indicates required field" renders by default if a top-level section and has required fields.
       },
     },
   };
   ```

   Note that `subblock` sections should be used as sub-sections within a `block` section.

   Using the `subblock` section with a `switch` toggle display indicates that the section toggle should act as a "master switch" for the properties within the section. In this case, the developer should:

   1. Create a property in the schema for the subblock section: something like `allow____` for what the section holds
   2. Add a `scope` field to the section in the uiSchema and let it be the property from step 1
   3. Create a rule that only shows the internal section properties when the section property is true, and add that rule to the uiSchema elements within the section
   4. Update the rendered component to ensure that the "master switch" property is adhered to and does not render

5. `accordion`

```ts
const uiSchema = {
  type: 'Section'
  options: {
    section: 'accordion',
    appearance: 'solid', // "solid" or "transparent" (optional)
    iconPosition: "end", // "start" or "end" (optional)
    iconType: "chevron", // "chevron" or "caret" or "plus-minus" (optional)
    scale: "m", // "m" or "s" or "l" (optional)
    selectionMode: "multiple" // "multiple" or "single" or "single-persist" (optional)
    elements: [
      {
        type: 'AccordionItem',
        label: "Label" // heading
        options: {
          expanded: true, // true or false, starting expanded state
          iconEnd: "globe", // any calcite-ui icon string
          iconStart: "globe", // any calcite-ui icon string
          description: {
            label: "label",
            labelKey: "description.label"
          },
          requiredHelperText: {
            label: "*indicates required field" // renders custom helper text at the top of the section to indicate what to do for required fields. "*indicates required field" renders by default if a top-level section and has required fields.
          }
        },
        elements: [
          ...
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

6. `card`

```ts
const uiSchema = {
  type: 'Section',
  options: {
    section: 'card',
    label: 'Expression' // title of card, can be labelKey instead
    actions: [ // actions to render in the footer-end of the card
    {
      alignment: 'start', // alignment of icon and text of action (optional)
      appearance: 'solid', // solid or transparent look (optional)
      compact: false, // make the action compact (optional)
      icon: 'trash', // calcite-icon to render (optional)
      key: 'expression-123', // a unique key that binds to the data-key attribute on the action (optional)
      label: 'Delete', // label to render on the action -- can also use labelKey (optional)
      onClick: (e) => { console.log(e) }, // on click handler for interacton (optional)
      scale: 's', // size to render action: s | m | l (optional)
      textEnabled: true, // whether to render the label on screen or just let it be aria-label,
      requiredHelperText: {
        label: "*indicates required field" // renders custom helper text at the top of the section to indicate what to do for required fields. "*indicates required field" renders by default if a top-level section and has required fields.
      }
    }
    ]
  }
}
```

Visit [the calcite documentation](https://developers.arcgis.com/calcite-design-system/components/accordion/) to understand the specific options on the accordion and accordion-item.

_NOTE:_ This section should be used instead of the block section as of now, as the block section does not pass a11y standards (it does not announce its label correctly nor its open/close state).

##### `type = "Slot"`

A `uiSchema` element with `type = "Slot"` allows us to render slotted content in the `arcgis-configuration-editor`. Within the `uiSchema` of your editor, you can define a dynamic slot element as follows:

```ts
{
  ...,
  elements: [
      {
        type: "Slot",
        options: { name: "slot-name" }
      }
  ]
}
```

When you define your editor, you can then slot content in under the provided slot name:

```html
<arcgis-configuration-editor ...>
  <div slot="slot-name">Slotted Content</div>
</arcgis-configuration-editor>
```

and when the `arcgis-configuration-editor` encounters an element of `type="Slot"` it will render

```jsx
<slot name={uiSchema.options.name} />
```

##### `type = "Notice"`

A `uiSchema` element with `type = "Notice"` allows us to render `arcgis-hub-notice` with the notice system in the `arcgis-configuration-editor`. Within the `uiSchema` of your editor, you can define a notice either through an id that references a pre-made notice in the `notices.ts` file, or by directly passing in a configuration in the `uiSchema`:

```ts
{
  ...,
  elements: [
    {
      type: "Notice",
      notice: IHubNotice,
      noticeId?: string
    }
  ]
}

// example of a pre-made notice

{
  ...,
  elements: [
    {
      type: "Notice",
      noticeId: "notice-123", // this id should exist in notices.ts
    }
  ]
}

// example of a notice configured directly in the uiSchema

{
  ...,
  elements: [
    {
      type: "Notice",
        notice: {
              configuration: {
                id: "config-harness-notice",
                noticeType: "notice",
                closable: false,
                kind: "info",
                scale: "m",
              },
              title: "An example of a notice.",
              message: "This notice was configured dynamically by passing in the notice configuration directly into the uiSchema.",
              autoShow: true,
              actions: [
                {
                  label: "Go to organization settings",
                  icon: "launch",
                  href: `${context.portalUrl}/home/organization.html?#settings`,
                  target: "_blank",
                },
              ],
            }
    }
  ]
}
```

When rendered, you should see `arcgis-hub-notice` in the DOM.

Please refer to the [notices documentation](https://github.com/ArcGIS/opendata-ui/blob/88c9e7964d7ea398490791b90ee3d8097384d63e/packages/hub-components/doc/notices.stories.mdx#L3) for more information about how to configure specific properties on notices.

#### rules

Rules allow us to conditionally render/disable fields in our editor.

`IUiSchemaRule` has the following properties:

| Properties   | Type     | Required? | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |
| ------------ | -------- | --------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `effect`     | `string` | yes       | What to do when the condition is met. Possible values are `SHOW`, `HIDE`, `DISABLE`, `ENABLE`, or `RESET`. Fields are shown and enabled by default unless rules are applied.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |
| `condition`  | `object` | no        | NOTE: this is deprecated. an object with scope (a jsonpointer that points to the json schema that this rule pertains to) and condition (an `IConfigurationSchema` that describes the rule).                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |
| `conditions` | `object` | no        | An array of conditions that determine if the rule effect should take place. Each condition can either be an object with scope (a jsonpointer that points to the json schema that this rule pertains to) and condition (an `IConfigurationSchema` that describes that rule), or it should be a simple boolean. This allows for outside factors to influence the evaluation of a rule, such as a `checkPermission` call. |

Note that `RESET` rules should usually be used in concjuntion with a DISABLE rule. In particular, RESET rules applied to text fields may not work precisely as expected unless there is also a `DISABLE` rule.

For example:

```js
{
  effect: 'HIDE',
  conditions: [
    {
      scope: '/properties/layout',
      schema: { const: 'moreInfo' }
    },
    checkPermission(...).access, // results in a boolean
  ]
}
```

_DEPRECATED WAY_:

```js
{
  effect: 'HIDE',
  condition: {
    scope: '/properties/layout',
    schema: { const: 'moreInfo' }
  }
}
```

When this rule is applied to a `uiSchemaElement`, that element will be hidden when all conditions are satisfied. Here, when the value of the `layout` property is "moreInfo", and the `checkPermission` call results in true, the element will be hidden. When the value of the `layout` property is any other value, or when the `checkPermission` call results in false, it will be shown. Since `schema` relies on jsonschema, complex conditions can be expressed.

The `arcgis-configuration-editor-field` is a child component of the `arcgis-configuration-editor` used to render the actual field controls based on the provided property `schema` and `uiSchema`.

In addition to rendering the actual field component, the `arcgis-configuration-editor-field` renders other shared content around the field, e.g. the field's label, helper text, and tooltip.

## Technical Implementation

The `arcgis-configuration-editor-field` receives all of the information for a single property in the editor, e.g. the property's name, current value, `schema`, `uiSchema`, and other prerequisites (i.e. validity, status, and requirement). 

### `uiSchema.options`
A property's `uiSchema` describes _how_ it should be rendered. Several pieces of information stored on a property's `uiSchema.options` are used by the `arcgis-configuration-editor-field` to render the field:

#### `uiSchema.options.control`

The `uiSchema.options.control` explicitly declares the tag name of the field component to dynamically render:

```ts
{
  ...
  type: 'Control'
  options: {
    control: 'hub-field-input-input'
  }
}
```

If a control is not specified, we provide logical fallbacks based on the property's `schema.type`:

| `schema.type` | default |
| ------------- | ------- | 
| `string` | `hub-field-input-input` | 
| `boolean` | `hub-field-input-switch` | 
| `array` | `hub-field-multi-select` |

#### `uiSchema.options.helperText`

The `uiSchema.options.helperText` is used to configure a field's helper text, which is located
between the field's label and the field itself.:

```ts
{
  ...,
  options: {
    helperText: {
      label: "some translated string",
      labelKey: 'some.label.key', // points to a translated helper text string
    }
  }
}
```

#### `uiSchema.options.messages`

The `uiSchema.options.messages` is an array of message objects, used to configure custom messages for controls in the schema. There are three types of messages: `ERROR`, `SUCCESS`, and `CUSTOM`. Each message can have an optional custom text provided either through a labelKey or a label. The message can also have an optional icon placed next to it. Error messages must have the `keyword` property to indicate from what invalid property the error message should render. Custom messages must have the `conditions` property object to determine when the message should render. `conditions` can be an array of either `IUiSchemaCondition` where the condition relates directly to the editor values, or simple booleans (resulting from something like a `checkPermission` call). The `allowShowBeforeInteract` property, when set to true, will allow that message for that field to render before that field has been interacted with; this is useful for fields that require input when another field is interacted with. The message renders below the field itself. The `disableMessages` option can be set on the schema to disable all messages rendering.:

```ts
{
  ...,
  options: {
    // disableMessages: true, set to supress all messages
    messages: [
      {
        type: "ERROR", // ERROR, SUCCESS, or CUSTOM
        keyword: "type", // fire when the "type" keyword is the error
        labelKey: 'some.label.key', // text for message
        icon: true // determines whether or not an icon should be rendered
        allowShowBeforeInteract: true,
      },
      {
        type: "SUCCESS",
        label: "This is the success text!",
        icon: false,
      },
      {
        type: "CUSTOM",
        label: "This is custom text for custom message",
        icon: "analysis" // if CUSTOM type, name of icon to render
        conditions: [
          { // condition for custom message to render on
          scope: '/properties/errorsIconMessage',
          schema: { const: 'hello' }
          },
          checkPermission(...).access, // evaluates to a boolean
        ]
        // deprecated:
        condition: { // condition for custom message to render on
          scope: '/properties/errorsIconMessage',
          schema: { const: 'hello' }
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}
```







#### `uiSchema.options.styles`
**NOTE:** This option should be used SPARINGLY. Using custom styling too often takes away from the similarity of the editing experience throughout the app. It should be used for specific situations in which it is necessary.

The `uiSchema.options.styles` allows for custom styling for each specific instance of a field through css custom variables. To use the custom styling, there are three requirements, each explained in more detail below:
1. The uiSchema includes the `styles` option
2. The field rendered has the infrastructure to update `styles`
3. The field's css file has the custom variable in the styling options in the uiSchema utilized

1. The uiSchema includes the `styles` option
To include the styling option on a field in the uiSchema, it should be written as `--variableName: variableValue`. For instance, to set the maxHeight of a radio field to `20vh`, the uiSchema should be written as:

```ts
{
  ...,

   scope: '/properties/radio',
          type: 'Control',
          options: {
            control: 'hub-field-input-radio',
            styling: {
              '--maxHeight': '20vh',
            }
          }
}
```

Note that you can also use css rules without variables here, as the styles are applied to the host element, as so:

```ts
{
  ...,

   scope: '/properties/radio',
          type: 'Control',
          options: {
            control: 'hub-field-input-radio',
            styling: {
              'color': 'red', // this will be applied to the host as well
            }
          }
}
```

2. The field rendered has the infrastructure to update `styling`
As many fields currently do not have css files, the fields must be updated to leverage the custom styling mechanics. To do this, the field should have:

```tsx
import { IStyleParams } from '../resources';

@Element() element: HTMLElement;

@Prop() styles: IStyleParams;

// in the component render function
render() {
  return (
    <Host style={this.styles}>
    ...
    </Host>
  )
}
```

in its component declaration. It should also have a css class added to the `@Component` decorator with `styleUrl: 'fieldName.css`. 

The current fields that support custom styling are:
- image-picker
- gallery-picker
- radio

If you update a field to support styling, please update this list. 

3. The field's css file is set up to utilize the styles
The style rules are applied to the host element. If the UiSchema styling specified uses css variables, then the final step is to create the style rule in the css file that matches the specified variable name in the `uiSchema`. For the previous example of setting the maxHeight on the radio field, the css file should look like:

```scss

/**
host {
  --maxHeight: 20vh; // this is set on the host through our custom styling in the uiSchema; not actually written in the css file
}
*/

fieldset {
  max-height: var(--maxHeight); // we do this to make the css variable apply
}
```

This will configure the max height to the specified value in the `uiSchema`.

#### `uiSchema.options.tooltip`

The `uiSchema.options.tooltip` is used to configure a field's tooltip: 

```ts
{
  ...,
  options: {
    tooltip: {
      labelKey: 'some.label.key' // points to a translated tooltip string
    }
  }
}
```

#### `uiSchema.options.clearOnHidden`

The `uiSchema.options.clearOnHidden` property is used to clear the property's value when the field is hidden by a rule. 

```ts
{
  ...,
  options: {
    clearOnHidden: true,
  }
}
```


#### `uiSchema.options.rules`

Some fields allow for rules on individual options in your field -- for example, the radio field allows for specific radio options in the field to be shown/hidden/disabled/enabled based on other values in the form or on permissions. To leverage this option, you should pass in a rules array in the order of your options, like so: 

```ts
{
  ...,
  options: {
    rules: [
      [{
        effect: "SHOW", // the effect the option should take when the condition is true -- could be "SHOW", "HIDE", "ENABLE", "DISABLE"
        conditions: [
          {
            scope: '/properties/name', // points to a property in the schema
            schema: { const: "hello" } // the value the other property in the schema should be
          },
          // these conditions would be joined with an AND for the effect to be true
          {
            checkPermission(...).access // a simple boolean can also be used as a condition
          }
        ],
      }],
      [],
      []
    ]
  }
}
```

### Field changes

When the user updates the value of an input field, this component emits an `arcgisConfigurationEditorFieldChange` event. The event detail includes the new input value:

```ts
{
  detail: {
    property: 'title',
    value: ...
  }
}
```

The `arcgis-configuration-editor` listens for this event, performs validation against the `schema`, and emits the editor's updated values in an `arcgisConfigurationEditorChange` event. 

## Supported Fields

For more information on the currently supported fields, read the field-specific docs (**note**: links will not work from storybook): 

| Field | Field Tag |
| ---------- | --------- | 
| Alignment | [hub-field-input-alignment](./fields/alignment/readme.md)
| Boundary Picker | [hub-field-input-boundary-picker](./fields/boundary-picker/readme.md)
| Color Picker | [hub-field-input-color](./fields/color-picker/readme.md)
| Date Picker | [hub-field-input-date](./fields/date-picker/readme.md)
| Image Picker | [hub-field-input-image-picker](./fields/image-picker/readme.md)
| Input | [hub-field-input-input](./fields/input/readme.md)
| Multiselect | [hub-field-input-multiselect](./fields/multiselect/readme.md)
| Radio | [hub-field-input-radio](./fields/radio/readme.md)
| Radio Group | [hub-field-input-radio-group](./fields/radio-group/readme.md)
| Rich Text | [hub-field-input-rich-text](./fields/rich-text/readme.md)
| Select | [hub-field-input-select](./fields/select/readme.md)
| Switch | [hub-field-input-switch](./fields/switch/readme.md)

### Supporting a new field

Follow these steps to support a new field control in the configuration editor

1. create a new folder for the field control under `src/components/arcgis-configuration-editor/arcgis-configuration-editor-field/fields`

2. create a new .tsx file for the field control component. In its most simple form, this component should look something like:

    ```tsx
    import { Component, Event, EventEmitter, h, Prop } from '@stencil/core';
    import { IRenderParams } from '../resources';
    import { bind } from '../../../../../../utils/context';

    @Component({
      tag: 'hub-field-input-[control-name]',
    })
    export class ControlName {
      constructor() {
        bind(this, 'handle[InternalInputFieldName]Change');
      }

      @Prop() params: IRenderParams;

      /**
       * Every field component emits this event. The
       * arcgis-configuration-editor-field component
       * listens for these events to emit editor changes
       * up to the arcgis-configuration-editor
       */
      @Event() arcgisConfigurationEditorFieldInputChange: EventEmitter<string>;

      /**
       * Every field component will have some sort of handler
       * function to handle the event emitted from the internal
       * input component that is being wrapped/rendered. This
       * handler takes the relevant value from this event and
       * emits it up to the arcgis-configuration-editor-field
       */
      handleInternalInputFieldNameChange (evt: CustomEvent<void>): void {
        this.arcgisConfigurationEditorFieldInputChange.emit(evt.detail.value);
      }

      render () {
        return (
          <some-internal-input-field
            disabled={this.params.disabled}
            onInternalInputFieldNameChange={this.handleInternalInputFieldNameChange}
            value={this.params.value} />
        )
      }
    }
    ```

3. Add relevant documentation for this new field component
    - update the component readme.md
    - update the `arcgis-configuration-editor-field` storybook story to include an example for this field
    - update the `arcgis-configuration-editor` harness to include an example for this field

4. Add/update relevant tests for this new field component
    - create a "test" directory in this field's directory
    - add a new .spec.tsx file for this field's unit tests
    - add a new .e2e.page.ts file to build a page model for this field that can be leveraged in config editor E2E tests
    - add a mapping of this field's page model to the arcgis-configuration-editor's [page model](../../test/arcgis-configuration-editor.e2e.page.ts)
    - add a test for this new field to the comprehensive arcgis-configuration-editor [e2e test](../../test/arcgis-configuration-editor.e2e.ts)

# arcgis-hub-attributes-list

Renders a `calcite-accordion` and a collection of `calcite-accordion-item` components for the given `content` and `fields` properties. At the time of this writing, we use [Cedar Charts](https://github.com/Esri/cedar) wrapped in an [Ember](https://emberjs.com/) component.

**Note:** This is somewhat of a stop-gap implementation as we're not ready to port our chart components to [Stencil](https://stenciljs.com/) yet, so this component _currently_ renders dynamically named slots for each "chartable" field so that the client application can pass `chart` and `table` content into the appropriate tab within each `calcite-accordion-item`. Once our chart components are ported to [Stencil](https://stenciljs.com/), we will deprecate the dynamically named slots and this component will directly render the chart and table content. At that time, we should consider simplifying this component's property signature to accept `content` or `content-id` and derive `fields`.

**Example:**

```html
const content: IHubContent = {
  // ...
  layers: [
    {
      // ...
      fields: [
        {
          name: 'acres',
          type: 'esriFieldTypeDouble',
          alias: 'SUM_ACREAGE',
          domain: null,
        },
      ],
    },
  ],
  layer: {
    // ...
    fields: [
      {
        name: 'acres',
        type: 'esriFieldTypeDouble',
        alias: 'SUM_ACREAGE',
        domain: null,
      },
    ],
  },
};

const fields: any[] = [
  {
    name: 'acres',
    type: 'esriFieldTypeDouble',
    alias: 'SUM_ACREAGE',
    domain: null,
    simpleType: 'numeric',
    icon: 'calcite-ui-number',
    statistics: {
      duration: 138.2936840057373,
    },
    i18nType: 'Number',
    chartable: false,
  },
];

<arcgis-hub-attributes-list
  content={content}
  fields={fields}
>
  <div slot="acres-chart">
    Custom chart content for "acres" field
  </div>
  <div slot="acres-table">
    Custom table content for "acres" field
  </div>
</arcgis-hub-attributes-list>
```

