# arcgis-hub-event-hero

Hero component for displaying the details of an Event entity.

## Default Usage
```jsx
<arcgis-hub-event-hero           
  entity={IHubEvent}
  extent={IExtent}
  graphics={any[]}
  hero-title={string}
  showMap={boolean}
  thumbnailUrl={string} />
```

## Getting Started with Gallery

At it's core the `arcgis-hub-gallery` component takes an [`IQuery`](https://esri.github.io/hub.js/api/common/IQuery/) and uses that to execute a search request, and then renders the results in the specified layout.

In addition, the Gallery present a search input, allowing the user to add to the query. Facets are used to allow the user to add more specificity to the query.

Simple use in a component

```
  ...other component code...

  // Return query of Web Maps the current user owns
  get query(): IQuery {
    return {
      targetEntity: "item",
      filters: [
        {
          predicates: [
            {
              type: "Web Map",
              owner: this._context.currentUser.username
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  }


  renderGallery(): VNode {
    return (
      <arcgis-hub-gallery
          layout="grid"
          query={this.query}
        />
    )
  }
```

## Bulk actions in galleries

Bulk actions are actions that are to be performed on a subset (selection) of items in a gallery.

In order to implement bulk actions in a gallery a developer must do 3 things:

1. Provide a `selectionMode` prop to the gallery with a value of 'single' or 'multiple'.
2. Provide a `bulkActions` prop to the gallery that describes the bulk actions (see below).
3. Handle `arcgisHubGalleryBulkAction` events in the gallery to perform the appropriate action.

The bulkActions prop is an object with an optional `position` property ('bottom' or 'top') and an actions property (array of `IComponentAction` objects).

If not provided, `position` defaults to 'bottom' which means it floats at the bottom of the screen. A `position` of 'top' means it will be `position: sticky` at the top of the gallery list. Note: it is possible that we will want to enhance the 'bottom' option by making it mean "float at the bottom of the list unless the bottom of the list is out of the viewport, then float at the bottom of the viewport". I (Jupe) have a branch with this mostly working.

The IComponentAction objects at a minimum need a `name` (a name for the action - this will be on the event so the consumer can take the appropriate action) and `text` (a text label for the button). Actions can optionally have children and, if so, will be rendered as a dropdown. An IComponentAction object may also contain:
1. `args` - additonal arguments to send in the event payload so that the consumer can use them when performing the action.
1. `helperText` - additonal text to render when an action is in a dropdown (ie a child of a parent action).
1. `icon` - the `calcite-icon` to render on the action button.
1. `loading` - a boolean indicating whether the action button should be rendered with a loading indicator.

A `bulkActions` configuration might look like:

```ts
{
  actions: [
    {
      name: 'messageUsers',
      icon: 'envelope',
      text: 'Message users',
    },
    {
      name: 'removeUsersFromGroup',
      args: {
        groupId: '5f5970fcc7b042888e23156fda9c3c70'
      },
      icon: 'user-minus',
      text: 'Remove users',
    },
    {
      text: 'Change role',
      icon: 'user-key',
      children: [
        {
          name: 'changeRole',
          args: {
            role: 'manager'
          },
          text: 'Manger',
          helperText: 'Can add/remove content and invite/remove people (except for the owner).'
        },
        {
          name: 'changeRole',
          args: {
            role: 'member'
          },
          text: 'Member',
          helperText: 'Can see content within the team.'
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
};
```

Note that the "Change role" action does not have a `name` property but does have two children. Also note that its two children have the same `name`, "changeRole", but different args.

If a developer supplied the above `bulkActions` config to the gallery, they might then listen for the `arcgisHubGalleryBulkAction` thus:

```ts
async handleBulkAction (evt) {
  const { action, selection } = evt.detail;

  switch (action.name) {
    case 'messageUsers':
      this.messageUsers(selection);
      break;
    case 'removeUsersFromGroup':
      await this.removeUsers(selection);
      // since we just removed users from the group, we need to update the selection/refresh the gallery
      // refresh the gallery
      this.galleryRef.refresh();
      // or update the selection (this would cause a refresh too) - this assumes gallerySelection is bound to the gallery
      this.gallerySelection = { groupMember: [] };
      break;
    case 'changeRole':
      this.changeRole(selection, action.args);
      break;
  }
}
```

# arcgis-hub-gallery-picker

The Gallery Picker component is a reusable component that enables the users to choose and add entities at a variety of places across Hub, e.g. project edit, teams edit, content library, etc.

There are several key props and states -

`gallerySelection` is the selection of IDs we pass down to `arcgis-hub-catalog` and then `arcgis-hub-gallery`, which will then exclude those IDs in the search query so they won't appear in the picker when the it's re-opened. This won't happen until the "Add" button in the picker is clicked.

`currentSelection` is the current seletion we use when selecte/deselect an entity, it will always have the inclusive list of IDs selected in picker.

`showSelection` is a boolean that indicates whether we want to show the entities of the IDs in the `gallerySelection` to the results list in the Gallery Picker. When there are pre-selected entities in the consuming app, by setting `showSelection` to true, when the Gallery Picker is opened, those entities/cards will appear as selected. Deselecting those entities will remove them from the consuming app UI and their IDs from the `gallerySelection`, thus they will appear to the Gallery Picker as unselected whenever it's opened.

This is where we add the IDs inside the `gallerySelection` to be excluded from the search query in `arcgis-hub-gallery`.
```
if (!this.showSelection && this.gallerySelection && flattenArray(Object.values(this.gallerySelection)).length) {
  // If there is an entry for the gallery selection for the type of the current query
  // add it to the exclude ID list
  for (const [type, ids] of Object.entries(this.gallerySelection)) {
    if (type === qry.targetEntity) {
      qry.filters.push({
        predicates: [
          {
            id: {
              not: [...ids]
            }
          }
        ]
      });
    }
  }
}
```

