# hub-field-input-simple-list

The `hub-field-input-list` renders a list field

## Usage

 The following `schema` and `uiSchema` show how you might declare this type of field when building out your editor's `schema` and `uiSchema`. They represent the `schema` and `uiSchema` for a single property: 

### `schema`

```ts
{
  type: "array",
  items: {
    type: "object",
  }
}
```

### `uiSchema`

```ts
{
  ..., // see all IUiSchemaElement properties in arcgis-configuration-editor docs
  type: 'Control',  
  options: {
    ..., // see non-field specific options in arcgis-configuration-editor-field docs
    control: 'hub-field-input-list',
    allowEdit: true,
    allowHide: true,
    allowReorder: true,
    allowRemove: false,
    allowAdd: true,
  }
}
```

#### `uiSchema.options` 
`uiSchema.options` specific to `hub-field-input-list` 

| option | type | required | description | 
| ------ | -------- | ------- | ----------- |
| `allowEdit` | `boolean` | `false` | whether the label of each list item can be edited |
| `allowHide` | `boolean` | `false` | whether each list item can be hidden / shown |
| `allowReorder` | `boolean` | `false` | whether each list item can be reordered |
| `allowRemove` | `boolean` | `false` | whether each list item can be removed |
| `allowAdd` | `boolean` | `false` | whether a new list item can be added, using the same edit schema/uiSchema
| `editSchema` | `IConfigurationSchema` | `false` | a JSONschema used to render the editor to edit a single item. Overrides the default editSchema if provided.
| `editUiSchema` | `IUiSchema` | `false` | a `uiSchema` used to render the editor to edit a single item. Overrides the default editUiSchema if provided.
| `editProp` | `string` | `false` | the property under which any item's edited values should be saved. if this is undefined, edited values are saved at the item's top-level.
| `hiddenEditProp` | `string` | `undefined` | An optional prop to use to give a different path for where the hidden value should be stored. This is useful if the list item has a hidden prop that is not at the top level, or is at the top level, but the rest of the list item values are stored within an editProp or scopeWrapperProp.
| `addItemLabel` | `string` | `undefined` | the label that is used for the "Add item" button. Defaults to "Add item".
| `scopeWrapperProp` | `string` | `undefined` | Optional scope prop to wrap the list item values in. This is done so that a composite field can be used to edit values. This should be the value of the single composite field used in the editSchema, if utilized. This wraps the values going into the component, and is used to pull values out of the change event; this prop does not stay on the internal list component's values.
| `editMode` | `modal`, `flow` | `false` | the mode of editing experience for editing an item. `modal` opens the editing experience in a modal with a configuration-form. `flow` opens the experience in a `calcite-flow-item`. 
| `flowTitle` | `string` | `false` | the translated heading of the calcite-flow-item when in the `flow` edit mode.
| `calciteFlowRefCallback` | `() => HTMLCalciteFlowElement` | `false` | a callback that returns a reference to a `calcite-flow` already in the DOM. This is only needed if the `editMode` is `flow` AND the user wants to render the list experience in the 2nd "stage" or later of the `calcite-flow`.
| `variant` | `CONFIGURATION_VARIANTS` | `false` | a variant type to pass to the editor that renders the editing experience for a single item, allowing the design of the editor to follow a variant's pre-defined style rules

## Editing an item in the list
This field supports editing an item in the rendered list. Currently, this can be done in two formats: 
- a modal
- a calcite-flow

In the modal experience, when the item's edit pencil is pressed, a modal is launched rendering a `arcgis-configuration-form`. This renders the `editSchema` and `editUiSchema` for the items. A `save` and `cancel` button are also rendered, so changes are not emitted until the `Save` button is pressed.

In the flow experience, when the item's chevron-arrow action is pressed, a `calcite-flow-item` should open with the editor for the selected item. If a `calciteFlowRefCallback` was passed in, then the `calcite-flow-item` will be rendered at the level of the targeted `calcite-flow` already in the DOM. If not, then the list field will render its own `calcite-flow` element. 

To build the editor for editing a specific item, you can either use the default `editSchema` and `editUiSchema` or pass in your own. The default `editSchema` only allows editing the item's label. 

To pass in your own `editSchema` and `editUiSchema`, you can do the following. This is an example to render a list that has two editable properties per item, one a string field and one a number field: 

```ts
const schema = {
  type: "object",
  properties: {
    list: { // our top level list property
      type: "array"
    }
  }
}
const uiSchema = {
  type: "Layout",
  elements: [
    scope: "/properties/list" // our top level list property
    type: "Control",
    options: {
      control: "hub-field-input-list",
      ..., // other options
      allowEdit: true,

      // schema that should be used when editing a specific item
      editSchema: {
        type: "object",
        properties: {
          itemProperty1: {
            type: "string"
          },
          itemProperty2: {
            type: "number"
          },
          ... // etc
        }
      }

      // uiSchema that should be used when editing a specific item
      editUiSchema: {
        type: "Layout",
        elements: [
          {
            type: "Control",
            scope: "/properties/itemProperty1"
          },
          {
            type: "Control",
            scope: "/properties/itemProperty2",
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
}

```

# hub-field-input-location-picker

The `hub-field-input-location-picker` component is a form field that allows the user to select a location from a map.

## Usage

The following `schema` and `uiSchema` show how you might declare this type of field when building out your editor's `schema` and `uiSchema`. They represent the `schema` and `uiSchema` for a single property:

## Schema

```ts
{
  type: 'object',
}
```

## uiSchema

```ts
{
  ..., // see all IUiSchemaElement properties in arcgis-configuration-editor docs
  type: 'Control',  
  options: {
    ..., // see non-field specific options in arcgis-configuration-editor-field docs
    control: 'hub-field-input-location-picker',
    extent: {
        "xmin": -88,
        "ymin": 39.1,
        "xmax": -87.10000000000001,
        "ymax": 40,
        "type": "extent",
        "spatialReference": {
        "wkid": 4326
        }
    },
    options: [
        {
            label: "No Location",
            location: {
                type: "none",
            }
        },
        {
            label: "Organization's Extent",
            description: "City of X",
            selected: true,
            location: {
                type: "org",
                extent: [[-88, 39.1], [-87.10000000000001, 40]],
                spatialReference: {
                wkid: 4326
                }
            }
        },
        {
            label: "Custom",
            description: "Draw one or more locations",
            location: {
                type: "custom",
                extent: [[-88, 39.1], [-87.10000000000001, 40]],
                spatialReference: {
                wkid: 4326
                }
            }
        },
    ]
}
}
```

### `uiSchema.options`
`uiSchema.options` specific to `hub-field-input-location-picker`

| option | type | required | description |
| ------ | ---- | -------- | ----------- |
| `extent` | `object` | `false` | extent to use for the map |
| `options` | `array` | `false` | array of options for user to select from |

**`options` interface:**
Base on `IHubLocationOption` interface
```ts
{
  /** Whether or not this option is selected initially */
  selected?: boolean;
  // The label that appears in the calcite-list
  label: string;
  // The description that appears in the calcite-list
  description?: string;
  // The location
  location: IHubLocation;
}
```
